        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f7bad994-a117-545d-8c08-8d7b11b51961",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Virgilia, Koeleria\n- Insects: Leto venus, Cyrtolabulus\n- Places: Southern Africa, Antarctica, Africa, Indomalayan region, Palearctic region\n- Concepts: Fabaceae, grass family, larval food plants, potter wasps\n\nFacts:\n- Virgilia is a genus of Southern African trees in the family Fabaceae.\n- Virgilia trees have shiny to hairy pinnate leaves, mauve to pink pea-shaped flowers, and leathery pods.\n- Koeleria is a common and widespread genus of plants in the grass family.\n- Koeleria is found on all continents except Antarctica and on various oceanic islands.\n- Leto venus uses Virgilia as its larval food plant.\n- Cyrtolabulus is a genus of potter wasps found in the African, Indomalayan, and Palearctic regions.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
